			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842369">
									

<p>(Can't give greenlights)</p>
<p>While interesting, the idea that the locket causes a person to be destined to defeat some objective evil is a bit too simplistic. Especially considering that evil is most often a matter of perspective. From the Foundation's point of view, their actions are completely justified, and other GoIs or SCPs are considered 'evil'.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Some&nbsp;more&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Less&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Which is especially difficult due to catch two, the Foundation meets all the requirements to be a villain. So their top priority is obviously making sure they never get selected as a target.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do the foundation know that they meet the requirements to be villains? Has the locket caused a person to go after another GoI or foundational personnel in the past? What are the requirements to be a villain? This sentiment would probably work better if the foundation was trying to keep the locket out of the hands of someone who <em>considers</em> the foundation evil.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For instance, what happens if you have someone touch the locket, then you terminate them? (Spoiler alert, the same thing that happens when the protagonist of a book is facing down a firing squad, it doesn’t end well for the firing squad).<br />
<br />
The person affected by the locket is both incapable of dying and incapable of killing people.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is an interesting and somewhat comedic idea, but it makes the locket somewhat generic and similar to other reality altering/ fate changing SCPs out there. Also, it brings up the question as to why the foundation doesn't use the locket's powers to dismantle other, more destructive, organizations. While the foundation isn't perfect, there are various cults and GoIs that cause more human suffering and chaos the they do.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, an interesting idea. The foundation trying to fight fate is a fairly interesting idea, but the properties of the locket could use some refinement. This could be a great narrative to explore what constitutes 'evil', but just making it something that wants to take down the foundation is a bit boring.</p>

							</div>

								
